# **The contested object, won**, and the last of the five transcribed entries to
# get an input. Its twin is `movies/level45-contested.zmv`, which is the same
# object lost.
#
# BLHR is level 45. The target is the type-$3A object at (230,1044) -- collision
# id $30, jump-table entry `$80:FAA4`: kind 3, and $1000 of score. Six earlier
# routes reached its box or its corridor and every one found it already gone,
# taken by a monster patrolling there.
#
# **It needed two changes to `tools/fit_route.py`, and neither is enough alone.**
#
# `--fire` alone arrived at frame 3712; the object was gone at 3466. Then the
# fitter learned to end a leg where the player *stopped* rather than letting the
# padded replay window expire -- level 45's route travelled 1,838 px against an
# 1,808 px optimum, so its slack was never detours, it was **263 frames of
# standing still** in blocks of 103, 43 and 43 at the ends of legs that had walked
# into a wall. That is worth 151 frames. But the stall fix *without* `--fire`
# arrives at **3489** -- earlier than this movie does -- and still loses: at frame
# 3460 the object is live, a monster is standing on it at (222,1046), the player is
# forty pixels below at (222,1086), and by 3470 it is gone. He watches it happen.
#
# So the clock is necessary and not sufficient, and the difference is visible in
# the display list. At frame 3550 here the object is still at (230,1044), the
# player is eight pixels below it at (222,1052) -- inside the box -- and the two
# id-$04 monsters nearby are at (234,1052) and (244,1052), **beside him rather
# than on it**, with one of his own shots at (222,1028). Shooting keeps the thief
# interested in the player instead of the object.
#
# The proof it landed is one word of WRAM: `zamn_headless --records` reads
# `score 00001000` at frame 3600, and $1000 is what this entry awards and nothing
# else in the game does.
#
# It is also the only movie that takes **all three of `$80:CAEE`'s exits at
# once** -- `object_taken` 19, `object_spent` 2, `object_ignore` 196 -- because a
# firing route puts bullets over objects, walks over one that a monster claimed
# first, and collects three of its own.
#
# `zamn_cosim verify -f 3600` checks 76,127 calls with no divergence, and 48 of
# the 111 marked branches.

